linux-wallpaperengine - GNOME Shell (Hidamari-style) backend
============================================================

This backend runs the full Wallpaper Engine rendering core (scenes, shaders,
particles, video and web wallpapers) completely offscreen and hands each frame
to a GNOME Shell extension through a POSIX shared memory segment. The extension
paints the frames inside Mutter's background group.

It does not use wlr-layer-shell and does not switch to Xorg, which makes it work
on GNOME 50 Wayland despite Mutter not implementing the layer-shell protocol.


Requirements
------------
- GNOME Shell 50 (Wayland)
- The engine built with -DGNOME_SHELL_BACKEND=ON (the default)


Building
--------
    cmake -S . -B build -DGNOME_SHELL_BACKEND=ON
    cmake --build build -j


Installing
----------
    ./packaging/gnome-shell/install.sh [engine-binary] [wallpaper-path]

The first argument is the path to the linux-wallpaperengine binary (optional,
auto-detected when omitted). The second is the wallpaper to use (optional, edit
the generated systemd unit if you leave it out).

Then enable the extension and start the engine:

    gnome-extensions enable linux-wallpaperengine-gnome@local
    systemctl --user enable --now linux-wallpaperengine-gnome.service

On Wayland you must log out and back in (or restart GNOME Shell) before the
extension is loaded.

You can also run the engine manually:

    linux-wallpaperengine --gnome-shell /path/to/wallpaper


Per-monitor wallpapers
----------------------
By default one wallpaper is rendered over the whole desktop bounding box. To give
every monitor its own wallpaper, repeat --gnome-shell-screen with the monitor
index (the engine logs them, 0-based) followed by --bg, and optionally
--scaling/--clamp:

    linux-wallpaperengine \
        --gnome-shell-screen 0 --bg 123456 --scaling fill \
        --gnome-shell-screen 1 --bg 789012 --scaling fill

Monitors have no connector name in GNOME Shell's JS API, so screens are picked by
index. A wallpaper is only rendered for monitors the extension reports.


Uninstalling
------------
    ./packaging/gnome-shell/uninstall.sh


How it works
------------
1. The engine creates an EGL surfaceless OpenGL context (falling back to a
   hidden GLFW window when EGL is unavailable) and renders the whole desktop
   bounding box into its own framebuffer.
2. Each frame is read back with glReadPixels into a double-buffered shared
   memory segment protected by a seqlock, exposed as
   /dev/shm/lwe-gnome-<pid>-<n>.
3. A lightweight FrameReady D-Bus signal wakes the extension, which reads the
   stable buffer and pushes it into an St.ImageContent on an actor inside the
   background group.
4. The extension reports the monitor layout and pointer position back to the
   engine over D-Bus so multi-monitor layouts and mouse-driven parallax work.


Known limitations
-----------------
- `Main.layoutManager._backgroundGroup` and the `rgba(0,0,0,0.01)` style hack
  are not public API; this backend targets GNOME Shell 50 only.
- Wallpapers cannot receive mouse clicks: an actor in the background group does
  not get pointer events. Only mouse position (parallax) is available.
- Fullscreen pause does not work: Mutter does not implement
  zwlr-foreign-toplevel-management, so there is no way to detect fullscreen
  windows. The extension still pauses rendering while the session is locked.
- CEF web wallpapers need a working headless/offscreen setup; verify them
  separately if you use them.
- Reading the frames is not zero-copy: GJS turns the GBytes returned by
  GLib.MappedFile.get_bytes() into a copy taken when the file is mapped, so the
  extension reads the segment from the file on every frame instead.